WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.491
Tokens per second measures generation rate,

00:00:02.491 --> 00:00:04.882
while prompt-processing speed measures input evaluation.

00:00:04.882 --> 00:00:09.266
Time to first token and end-to-end latency matter for interactive use.

00:00:09.266 --> 00:00:12.055
Throughput matters for batches and multiple users.

00:00:12.055 --> 00:00:15.642
Splitting one model can increase capacity without increasing speed.

00:00:15.642 --> 00:00:19.228
Each token may cross device boundaries or require synchronization.

00:00:19.228 --> 00:00:20.822
If communication dominates compute,

00:00:20.822 --> 00:00:25.206
adding a GPU can leave performance unchanged or make it worse.

00:00:25.206 --> 00:00:29.988
PCI Express bandwidth and topology affect movement among CPU memory and GPUs.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.574
Devices may share lanes or connect through different paths.

00:00:33.574 --> 00:00:37.559
Fast peer-to-peer features are hardware, platform, driver, and runtime dependent.

00:00:37.559 --> 00:00:41.943
GPU utilization should be interpreted with memory, clocks, power, temperature, transfer,

00:00:41.943 --> 00:00:43.139
and queue data.

00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:47.921
One GPU at low utilization may be waiting for another device or

00:00:47.921 --> 00:00:50.312
for communication rather than lacking work.

00:00:50.312 --> 00:00:53.500
Thermal throttling and power limits reduce sustained performance.

00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:56.289
Test long enough to reach stable temperatures.

00:00:56.289 --> 00:01:01.868
A short benchmark after a cold boot can hide airflow imbalance and power behavior.

00:01:01.868 --> 00:01:06.252
Measure the intended workload: model, quantization, context, batch, users, prompt length,

00:01:06.252 --> 00:01:07.846
output, and service layer.

00:01:07.846 --> 00:01:11.432
Compare single GPU, different splits, replicas, and separate-job placement.

00:01:11.432 --> 00:01:14.620
Multi-GPU earns its complexity only through measured benefit.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.410
Report variability and percentiles, not only averages.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.598
Synchronization stalls, model loading, cache growth, queue bursts,

00:01:20.598 --> 00:01:25.380
and thermal behavior may create occasional long responses that dominate user experience.
